experiment: f3seq
  SHORT RUN — 240000 ticks against a minimum of 1200000.   §48: SEQUENTIAL teaching. §47 taught three axes AT ONCE and the
  three-axis code lost -- breaking nine-axes note 4, which says teaching
  stays SEQUENTIAL because two dimensions share one reward channel. Four
  arms: the 2x2x2 taught one axis per session third at x1 and x3 budget,
  plus §47's SIMUL and STRETCH arms UNCHANGED as reproduction checks.
  Window is the FINAL THIRD, so F1 is the oldest lesson and the primary
  is also a retention test. Clause 3 counts feedback per third, because a
  schedule that never switched would make a sequential arm a one-axis
  lesson and its null a false refusal. Tier::kLong
  Everything below is printed for debugging and is NOT a
  measurement; this run cannot pass whatever it reads.

  the question      §48. §47 taught three axes AT ONCE and a three-axis
                    code lost. nine-axes note 4 says teaching stays
                    SEQUENTIAL. This teaches them one at a time.
  the mechanism     axispower: a lesson SILENT about another's axis costs
                    it nothing (+11.5 SE). Per-axis scoring makes the three
                    lessons silent by construction.
  the window        delivered |d| between each axis's EXTREME levels over
                    the FINAL THIRD -- so F1 is the OLDEST lesson in the
                    sequential arms and the primary is also a RETENTION
                    test. Whole-session is printed for §47's check.
  disclosed         the order is FIXED at F1 -> F2 -> f3, so "sequential"
                    is confounded with "f3 last". All three axes are
                    reported separately so that shows as an asymmetry.
  render_f3_gain    1.00
  context_source    4

  instrument        f3seq   trial seed 000000000135d281   36 creatures
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
